  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: [{
    '@type': 'Question',
    name: 'Who owns the compatible-capability lifecycle?',
    acceptedAnswer: {
      '@type': 'Answer',
      text: 'Decision 127 (accepted November 6, 2026): runtime.wasm owns the compatible-capability lifecycle on every host, including web. It owns instance ids, state validation, ordered lifecycle events, shutdown cleanup, Spec 1722 canonical start input, and the platforms[] allowlist. On traverse main, #1822 implements it in runtime.wasm and amends Spec 072 to 1.1.0, and #1824 (Decision 128) switches Swift, Kotlin, .NET, and web onto it, with a breaking change to web error codes. The Rust traverse-embedder follows under Decision 129 (#1823, #1828 open). Published Traverse v0.16.2 still stubs traverse_compatible_start/stop/kill to return -1, and web still runs its own TypeScript lifecycle. Not in a tagged release yet.',
